Exactly. Monsters placed on buildings to scare away other monsters. 😉

A scarecrow protects a field by imitating a predator.

A gargoyle, in one traditional interpretation, protects a building by embodying the very evil it’s supposed to repel.

And there’s a beautiful inversion there:

You give the thing you fear a face, then put that face to work protecting you.

Historically, true gargoyles also functioned as rainwater spouts, while similar sculptures without spouts are called grotesques. Their frightening imagery has often been interpreted as protective, or apotropaic.

Now abstract the principle:

Represent the threat → make the representation visible → use it to influence the threat itself.

That works beyond architecture. Warning coloration, decoys, camouflage, deterrence, symbols of authority, even reputation.

Sometimes the monster at the gate isn’t there to keep you in.

It’s there to keep something else out.

